.SH KEYS
.TP
\fBbindings\fR
The key bindings, beside the built\-in ones. One on keys a built\-in binding uses, or that begin with them, takes its place.
.TP
\fBbindings[].action\fR
What the binding does.
.RS
.TP
\fBclose\fR
Ask the focused window to close.
.TP
\fBclose_program\fR
Ask every window of the program the focused window is of to close, as its close button would, so that the program may ask to save: as Cmd+Q quits a program on a Mac. Its program is the windows that share its application's name; one that has none is a program of its own.
.TP
\fBmaximize\fR
Toggle the focused window between maximized and its normal size.
.TP
\fBfullscreen\fR
Toggle fullscreen for the focused window.
.TP
\fBminimize\fR
Hide the focused window.
.TP
\fBhide_program\fR
Hide every window of the program the focused window is of, on any workspace, as Cmd+H does on a Mac.
.TP
\fBhide_others\fR
Hide every window of every program but the focused window's, as Cmd+Option+H does on a Mac.
.TP
\fBshow_desktop\fR
Set every window of the workspace shown aside, to show the desktop, as a Mac does: each slid straight out from the middle of the screen until a sliver is left at an edge, not minimized, and back where it was once this is asked again, Escape is pressed, a window or its sliver is chosen, or one opens, or as its workspace is left.
.TP
\fBshade\fR
Roll the focused window up into its titlebar, or let it down again.
.TP
\fBkeep_above\fR
Keep the focused window above the others, or stop keeping it there.
.TP
\fBstick\fR
Have the focused window on whichever workspace its screen is showing, or leave it on the one it is on.
.TP
\fBfocus_next\fR
Focus the next window on the workspace.
.TP
\fBfocus_previous\fR
Focus the previous window on the workspace.
.TP
\fBswitch_windows\fR
Open the switcher at the window used before the one in use, or, while it is open, go on to the next. Letting go of the keys held with it chooses the window it is at.
.TP
\fBswitch_windows_back\fR
The same, going back: the switcher opens at the window used longest ago.
.TP
\fBswitch_programs\fR
Open the switcher at the program used before the one in use, as Cmd+Tab does: each program once, by its window used last; or, while it is open, go on to the next. The program chosen comes forward with all its windows on the workspace.
.TP
\fBswitch_programs_back\fR
The same, going back: the switcher opens at the program used longest ago.
.TP
\fBnext_window\fR
With keys held, open the switcher on the focused window's program's windows, as Cmd+backquote does: a quick press goes back to the one used before. With the switcher open, go through the windows of the program it has picked, in it. With no keys held, focus the next window of the program on its workspace, going round.
.TP
\fBprevious_window\fR
The same, going back: with no keys held, the window of the program before the focused one on its workspace, going round.
.TP
\fBoverview\fR
Open the overview, every window of the workspace side by side under the workspaces of the screen, to pick one from; or close it.
.TP
\fBshow_program_windows\fR
Open the overview with only the windows of the focused window's program on its workspace, as a Mac's App Expos\[u00E9] shows them, to pick one from; or close the overview, whichever windows it shows.
.TP
\fBnext_tab\fR
Switch to the tab after the active one in the focused window's group.
.TP
\fBprevious_tab\fR
Switch to the tab before the active one in the focused window's group.
.TP
\fBnew_workspace\fR
Make a workspace after the last and switch to it. It goes again once it is empty and another is shown.
.TP
\fBnext_workspace\fR
Switch to the following workspace.
.TP
\fBprevious_workspace\fR
Switch to the preceding workspace.
.TP
\fBmove_to_new_workspace\fR
Send the focused window to a workspace made for it, after the last.
.TP
\fBmove_to_next_output\fR
Send the focused window to the next screen.
.TP
\fBshow_menu\fR
Open the root menu at the pointer.
.TP
\fBshow_launcher\fR
Open the launcher, to start a program by typing its name.
.TP
\fBshow_programs\fR
Open the launcher onto every program installed, as a grid of their icons with their names, to pick one from or narrow by typing, as a Mac shows its Apps. If the launcher is open already, it is left as it is.
.TP
\fBfocus_notifications\fR
Give the notifications the keyboard, the newest first: Tab and the arrow keys go through it and its buttons, Enter chooses, as a click does, and Escape dismisses it.
.TP
\fBshow_welcome\fR
Show the welcome card: a step at a time, what the keys that matter do, light or dark, the wallpapers that come with omorfos, and the applications that open what. It is shown by itself at the first login.
.TP
\fBabout\fR
Show "About omorfos": its version, and what it is made with that is someone else's. Asked again while it is shown, it goes.
.TP
\fBlock\fR
Lock the session, with the program \fBlocker\fR in config.yaml names.
.TP
\fBsleep\fR
Put the machine to sleep, as \fBomorfos\-session sleep\fR does.
.TP
\fBrestart\fR
Start the machine again, as \fBomorfos\-session restart\fR does: once the user has said so, unless \fBpower.ask\fR in config.yaml is false.
.TP
\fBshut_down\fR
Turn the machine off, as \fBomorfos\-session shut\-down\fR does: once the user has said so, unless \fBpower.ask\fR in config.yaml is false.
.TP
\fBlog_out\fR
End the session, as \fBquit\fR does: once the user has said so, unless \fBpower.ask\fR in config.yaml is false.
.TP
\fBstay_awake\fR
Keep the session from locking, the screens from going off and the machine from sleeping when the user is away, until asked again; or, while it is kept so, let it go.
.TP
\fBnight_light\fR
Make the screens warm now if they are not, as warm as \fBnight_light\fR says, or as they are if they are, until night light next turns them by the time of day.
.TP
\fBnext_layout\fR
Type with the next of the keyboard's layouts, and after the last with the first again.
.TP
\fBnext_screens\fR
Have the screens stand the next way: as the configuration says, all showing what the first shows, and each alone. Each is kept only if the user says so in time.
.TP
\fBguess_scales\fR
Guess anew the scale for every screen plugged in, in place of the one guessed when each was first seen, and keep that from now on: as a newer version of omorfos would guess. A scale \fBconfig.yaml\fR gives is laid over it still.
.TP
\fBreload_config\fR
Re\-read the configuration now.
.TP
\fBrestart_compositor\fR
Start the compositor again, keeping the login, as \fBomorfos\-hold\fR does when it ends: the newest version installed. Programs stay open through it, their windows where they were, but for programs written for X11, which close with their X server.
.TP
\fBquit\fR
End the session.
.RE
